<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Trentino</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Province of Trento</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Trentino is a mountainous region of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> in the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adige">Adige</a> valley, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trento">Trento</a> as its capital, today an autonomous province. For most of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> and early modern period much of it was ruled by the prince-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> of Trent. Secularised in 1803, it then belonged to the Austrian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyrol">Tyrol</a> until 1918, when Italy occupied it after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>; the annexation was confirmed in 1919.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/trentino.jpg" alt="Trentino" width="320" height="402" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: TUBS · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Trentino_in_Italy.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
